WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:05.920
For the purposes of a report or to show&nbsp;
a scenario, one may want to show data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:11.200
in a calculated form. Take, for example, this&nbsp;
query that shows some information on products,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:15.600
including the product's list price. Say&nbsp;
we want to add a column that will show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:20.160
the list price minus 10% as a&nbsp;
column titled 10% Off Price.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:27.840
I will add a comma after the existing List Price&nbsp;
field and then add the ListPrice column again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:36.320
this time multiplying it by .9 to take 10% off&nbsp;
the price, and then I will add a column alias of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:45.920
'10% Off Price' in quotes. I will execute this&nbsp;
statement, and we see that the query works.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:54.160
However, on a second inspection of the pricing&nbsp;
column, it would be nice if the dollar amounts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:58.640
looked like dollar amounts, carried to two places.&nbsp;
There are a few ways to format this number,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:05.360
but the easiest way is to add the string&nbsp;
function (str) to this new, computed column.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:11.840
I will add the letters str and an open&nbsp;
parenthesis before the column, then 8,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:21.440
2, and close parenthesis to indicate&nbsp;
eight characters long with two numbers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:34.560
after the decimal point. Let's see how this&nbsp;
looks when I execute it. It looks good.

